Task 1: Configure Node Affinity Rules for VMs
Node affinity allows you to constrain which nodes your VMs can be scheduled on based on labels and node characteristics. This is crucial for ensuring VMs run on appropriate hardware.

Subtask 1.1: Examine Current Node Configuration
First, let's explore the existing nodes and their labels to understand our scheduling options.

Step 1: List all nodes in the cluster

kubectl get nodes
Step 2: View detailed node information with labels

kubectl get nodes --show-labels
Step 3: Examine node capacity and allocatable resources

kubectl describe nodes
Step 4: Check for existing node labels that we can use for affinity

kubectl get nodes -o json | grep -A 10 -B 10 "labels"
Subtask 1.2: Add Custom Labels to Nodes
We'll add custom labels to categorize nodes based on their intended use.

Step 1: Label nodes for different workload types

# Label first node for high-performance workloads
kubectl label nodes <node-1-name> workload-type=high-performance

# Label second node for standard workloads
kubectl label nodes <node-2-name> workload-type=standard

# Label third node for development workloads
kubectl label nodes <node-3-name> workload-type=development
Step 2: Add hardware-specific labels

# Add CPU type labels (replace with actual node names)
kubectl label nodes <node-1-name> cpu-type=intel-xeon
kubectl label nodes <node-2-name> cpu-type=amd-epyc
kubectl label nodes <node-3-name> cpu-type=intel-core

# Add memory capacity labels
kubectl label nodes <node-1-name> memory-size=large
kubectl label nodes <node-2-name> memory-size=medium
kubectl label nodes <node-3-name> memory-size=small
Step 3: Verify labels have been applied

kubectl get nodes --show-labels | grep workload-type
Subtask 1.3: Create VM with Required Node Affinity
Now we'll create a virtual machine with node affinity rules that specify where it should be scheduled.

Step 1: Create a VM configuration file with node affinity

nano high-performance-vm.yaml
Step 2: Add the following configuration

apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: high-performance-vm
  namespace: default
spec:
  running: false
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: high-performance-vm
    spec:
      affinity:
        nodeAffinity:
          requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
            nodeSelectorTerms:
            - matchExpressions:
              - key: workload-type
                operator: In
                values:
                - high-performance
              - key: cpu-type
                operator: In
                values:
                - intel-xeon
          preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
          - weight: 100
            preference:
              matchExpressions:
              - key: memory-size
                operator: In
                values:
                - large
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 4
        memory:
          guest: 8Gi
        devices:
          disks:
          - name: containerdisk
            disk:
              bus: virtio
          - name: cloudinitdisk
            disk:
              bus: virtio
          interfaces:
          - name: default
            masquerade: {}
        networks:
        - name: default
          pod: {}
      volumes:
      - name: containerdisk
        containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/cirros-container-disk-demo
      - name: cloudinitdisk
        cloudInitNoCloud:
          userDataBase64: SGkuXG4=
Step 3: Apply the VM configuration

kubectl apply -f high-performance-vm.yaml
Step 4: Verify the VM has been created

kubectl get vm
